Fiber executor (fpm-ng): non-blocking stream_select()

Everything below is compiled ONLY when HAVE_FPMNG_FIBER is defined —
fpm-ng's configure sets it for --enable-fpmng-fiber (sapi/fpmng/config.m4).
Without it this file is byte-for-byte upstream.

What changes when it is defined: PHP_FUNCTION(stream_select) delegates the
actual wait to fpm_fiber_select() (sapi/fpmng/fpm/fpm_pool_fiber_select.c)
instead of calling php_select()/select() directly. Outside a request running
on pool.executor = fiber — including CLI and every other pool type in the
same binary — fpm_fiber_select() falls back to the real, process-blocking
select() itself (fpm_pool_fiber_can_wait() is false there), so this patch
changes nothing observable when the fiber executor is not in use.

Weak declaration, not a header include: fpm_pool_fiber_select.c is linked
only into sapi/fpmng/php-fpm-ng (build/prepare.sh's FIBER_PATTERN), while
this file is compiled once into PHP_GLOBAL_OBJS and linked into every SAPI in
the tree (cli, cgi, phpdbg, ...). An ordinary #include + call would leave
those binaries with an undefined reference to fpm_fiber_select() the moment
--enable-fpmng-fiber is on — exactly the problem patch 0007 already solved
for ext/openssl the same way: a weak extern that resolves to NULL outside
fpm-ng's own binary, checked at the call site.

Why here and not at the transport layer, like the rest of the fiber
executor's socket interception (fpm_pool_fiber_xport.c): stream_select()
takes SETS of descriptors from userland — some wrapped by us, some not, some
not sockets at all — which the per-stream transport wrapper has no way to
see. See tasks/nice-to-have/006-fiber-stream-select.md for the analysis.

What this does NOT change: the exceptfds set (efds) still falls back to the
real select() call whenever it is non-empty — see fpm_fiber_select()'s
comment for why. Neither Predis nor php-amqplib, the two consumers this task
exists for, pass it.

Waits on: nothing upstream — fpm-ng behavior, gated so upstream is the
default. Expires by redesign (fiber executor dropped, or an upstream async
I/O layer appears).
Checked on: PHP-8.5 branch (67d1476d4d80, 8.5.11-dev).

diff --git a/ext/standard/streamsfuncs.c b/ext/standard/streamsfuncs.c
index 9b23600b..385c013c 100644
--- a/ext/standard/streamsfuncs.c
+++ b/ext/standard/streamsfuncs.c
@@ -28,6 +28,16 @@
 #include <unistd.h>
 #endif

+#ifdef HAVE_FPMNG_FIBER
+/* fpm-ng: pool.executor = fiber. fpm_pool_fiber_select.c is linked only into
+ * sapi/fpmng/php-fpm-ng (build/prepare.sh's FIBER_PATTERN), while this file is
+ * linked into every SAPI in the tree — the same problem patch 0007 solves for
+ * ext/openssl. A weak declaration keeps cli/cgi/phpdbg linkable: there the
+ * symbol resolves to NULL and the call below falls back to the real
+ * select(). */
+extern int fpm_fiber_select(int max_fd, fd_set *rfds, fd_set *wfds, fd_set *efds, struct timeval *timeout) __attribute__((weak));
+#endif
+
 #ifndef PHP_WIN32
 #define php_select(m, r, w, e, t)	select(m, r, w, e, t)
 typedef unsigned long long php_timeout_ull;
@@ -823,7 +833,12 @@ PHP_FUNCTION(stream_select)
 		}
 	}

+#ifdef HAVE_FPMNG_FIBER
+	retval = fpm_fiber_select ? fpm_fiber_select(max_fd+1, &rfds, &wfds, &efds, tv_p)
+		: php_select(max_fd+1, &rfds, &wfds, &efds, tv_p);
+#else
 	retval = php_select(max_fd+1, &rfds, &wfds, &efds, tv_p);
+#endif

